  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1252 PM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\n...NEAR TERM (TODAY THROUGH TUESDAY)...\nA pair of potent mid-level shortwaves -- one situated over the \nDesert Southwest and another over North Dakota -- will drive a \nslow-moving surface low and cold front across the region through\nthe first half of the week. Surface analysis this afternoon \nshows a 996 mb low over northeastern South Dakota, with a trough\naxis extending southwestward into a second low center of 998 mb\nover western Kansas. Strong southwesterly flow ahead of these \nsynoptic features is fueling robust warm and moist advection \nacross the Midwest. \n\nSouth-southeast winds gusting 20 to 30 mph today will boost \ntemperatures into the mid 60s today. Highs will warm further \ninto the low to mid 70s Tuesday as the pressure gradient \ntightens. Southerly wind gusts on Tuesday may approach 40 mph at\ntimes, with NBM probabilities for gusts exceeding 40 mph \nranging from 50 to 70% across a significant portion of the CWA. \nThe probability of reaching Wind Advisory criteria (45 mph \ngusts) is roughly 30 to 50% north of I-70. While confidence is \ncurrently insufficient to issue a Wind Advisory, a few localized\nsites may tag criteria. Meanwhile, sustained moisture transport\nwill push surface dewpoints into the low to mid 60s by Tuesday \nafternoon.\n\n...SEVERE WEATHER THREAT (TUESDAY AFTERNOON & EVENING)...\nAs the elongated, double-barrel surface low lifts across the \nmid and Upper Mississippi Valley Tuesday afternoon, weak \ninstability is forecast to overspread portions of central \nIllinois. This instability will be superimposed by strong deep-\nlayer shear of 40 to 50 kt, supporting a conditional severe \nweather threat Tuesday afternoon and evening should localized \ncloud breaks allow for greater surface heating. Even without \nrobust convective updrafts or lightning, the intense background \nsynoptic flow within the cloud-bearing layer could easily be \ntransferred to the surface, resulting in near-severe or \nmarginally severe wind gusts in convective downdrafts.\n\n...HEAVY RAIN & HYDROLOGY (TUESDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY)...\nPrecipitable water values exceeding 1.50 inches pooling along \nthe frontal zone approach maximum values in the 1985-2012 GEFS \nreforecast climatology. High rainfall efficiency is expected as \na slow-moving band of showers and isolated embedded \nthunderstorms traverses the forecast area Tuesday into \nWednesday. The 48-hour NBM mean QPF through Wednesday night \nindicates widespread totals of 0.75 to 1.50 inches, with 90th \npercentile ensemble guidance highlighting localized totals up to\n2.0 to 2.5 inches.\n\n...EXTENDED PERIOD (THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY)...\nCold frontal passage will bring an end to widespread moderate-\nto-heavy precipitation by late Wednesday night as the surface \nlow gradually fills and meanders across the Great Lakes. \nPersistent low-level cyclonic flow and wrap-around moisture \nwithin post-frontal cold air advection (CAA) will maintain cloud\ncover along with scattered sprinkles or light showers Thursday \nthrough Saturday, particularly as shortwave vorticity lobes \npinwheel southward around the departing upper trough. Post-\nfrontal high temperatures Friday through the weekend will cool \ninto the 50s, which remains near seasonal normals. Recent GFS \nand ECMWF guidance is showing better consensus regarding a fast-\nmoving shortwave diving across the region on Sunday, bringing \nthe next opportunity for more widespread light precipitation.",
